    "c2": {
      "anchor": "i313",
      "kind": "item",
      "label": "Test runs taking turns: one session's full run held the test turn 30+ minutes so others' waits gave up, and waiters aren't served first come, first served",
      "excerpt": "Test runs taking turns: one session's full run held the test turn 30+ minutes so others' waits gave up, and waiters aren't served first come, first served",
      "text": "So we should separate out tests that are timing sensitive into integration tests, of which we have only a few, right?",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-16T05:50:18.569Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-16T05:51:28.487Z",
          "html": "<p>Yes.  The slow ones are the tests that start real git and node processes (the worktree hook, the end of a turn, doc-links, the multi-repo tests) and a few browser timing tests:  a small set.</p><p>They go into their own integration project, run one at a time and in the turn.  The everyday test run an agent does after a change skips them, so it stays fast and needs no turn;  the full check and CI still run everything.</p><p>Agent epic-engine-turns-fair is doing it as part of I313.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-16T05:51:27.504Z"
      }
    }
  },
